# KS-1193 (F2 only) — Wednesday's task for Ornith, VITEST, **TEST-ONLY MODE, ONE NEW TEST FILE** (written 07:38 on 2026-09-17)

Written from origin develop `eb1051fd39fe3edab4e0b1d1967515b758d4ba3f` (#1015's squash, KS-1018). The product file
`Blockchain/Dev/services/auth/src/routes/users.ts` (1365 lines, blob `8ef9065e2`, the #1015 gate's equality target) was
read at `:1-40` and `:1120-1365`. `repositories/dbErrors.ts` (blob `f94faf0d3`) and `middleware/errorHandler.ts` (the
`NotFoundError` and `ServiceUnavailableError` codes) were read. The sibling test
`ks1018-security-correctness-three-verification-store-reads.test.ts` (blob `6723276d0`, 216 lines) was read whole; this
task copies its mock shape. The #1015 gate verdict, its report (F2, F4, the D-TW-GET row) and its `tampers.py` were read.
**PRE-MEASURED** in a scratch clone at the tip; see `## Premises (measured)`. **This is a PARTIAL of KS-1193:** F1 has its
own brief (`KS-1193-F1`); F3 (the memory fallback) is NOT briefed.

## THE MODE — read this twice

TEST-ONLY, ONE NEW FILE. Your diff contains EXACTLY ONE file: the NEW test file
`Blockchain/Dev/services/auth/src/__tests__/ks1193-review-read-query-error-is-not-503.test.ts`, written as
`--- /dev/null` then `+++ b/…`, one `@@ -0,0 +1,117 @@` hunk, every line `+`.
**Do NOT modify `routes/users.ts`**, and do NOT modify `ks1018-security-correctness-three-verification-store-reads.test.ts`
(the reference test). `users.ts` is in files[...] only so you can see the lines the new test reaches. The checker plants
the tamper named at the bottom into a clone of `users.ts` and expects the two 🔴 cells RED and the 🟢 control GREEN, then
expects all three GREEN at the untouched tip.

## What is wrong (one paragraph)

KS-1018 made the review route's by-id read (`getVerificationRequest`) rethrow ONLY infrastructure DB errors as 503
(`:1186`); a query-level pg error (a missing relation `42P01`, a serialization failure `40001`) is logged and the route
answers as before, which for an unknown request is `404 NOT_FOUND`. The GET and POST sites each have a KS-1018 control
that feeds a non-infrastructure error, so widening THEIR rethrow to every error goes red. The review site has none. So
the #1015 gate's D-TW-GET tamper (`if (err) throw new ServiceUnavailableError(…)` at `:1186`) passed 751 of 751 cells:
a missing table or a revoked grant on the review read would answer 503 "please retry" forever. **This task:** a NEW file
mounts the REAL `userRoutes` and the REAL `errorHandler`, has a SYSTEM_ADMIN review an unknown request while only the
by-id read rejects with a query-level pg error, and asserts the answer is NOT `SERVICE_UNAVAILABLE`, its status is 404 or
500, and nothing is written.

## Where (no product site changes — this list is informational; every line below STAYS)

- `:1185` — (correct) `      logger.error('DB getVerificationRequest failed', { error: err?.message, code: err?.code });` — the line the 🟢 control reads. Stays.
- `:1186` — (correct) `      if (isInfrastructureDbError(err)) throw new ServiceUnavailableError('Authentication service temporarily unavailable, please retry');` — the review read's rethrow. The tamper replaces this line. Stays.
- `:1189` — (correct) `  return memVerificationRequests.get(id) || null;` — the fall-through; for an unknown id it returns null. Stays.
- `:1319` — (correct) `    const request = await getVerificationRequest(requestId);` — the review route's only call of the read. Stays.
- `:1320` — (correct) `    if (!request) throw new NotFoundError('Verification request');` — the 404 the cells accept. Stays.

## The test — CREATE THE NEW FILE `Blockchain/Dev/services/auth/src/__tests__/ks1193-review-read-query-error-is-not-503.test.ts`

**Harness:** `vitest run` inside `Blockchain/Dev/services/auth` (`package.json` scripts.test is `vitest`;
`vitest.config.ts`: globals, node environment, `setupFiles: ['./vitest.setup.ts']`). To run this file alone:
`npx vitest run src/__tests__/ks1193-review-read-query-error-is-not-503.test.ts`.

The `vi.hoisted` state, the six `vi.mock` blocks and the imports are the ks1018 reference test's own shape (a recording
userRepo, an injected caller, `../db` with `isDbAvailable` true and `query` a `vi.fn`, a captured `logger`). The caller is
a SYSTEM_ADMIN in every cell. The server is mounted on `127.0.0.1:0`. `failReviewRead(err)` makes `query` reject with
`err` ONLY for the SELECT that carries `WHERE id = ` (the review by-id read) and answer every other statement healthily.
`writes()` returns `[level writes, request INSERTs]`. No network beyond 127.0.0.1, no database.

**Reproduce the body below EXACTLY as written.** It is 117 lines. Do not shorten it, do not replace any part with a
comment, do not invent a helper, do not rename a variable, do not collapse the cells into a loop or an `it.each`. Every
name a cell reads (`failReviewRead`, `review`, `writes`, `MISSING_TABLE`, `SERIALIZATION`, `GET_SQL`, `logger`,
`userRepo`) is declared or imported ABOVE the first cell. **The file contains no backslash anywhere and no template
literal; keep it that way.** Every string is single-quoted and contains no quote character, so no escape is needed.

```ts
// KS-1193 F2 (the #1015 gate on KS-1018): the review read has no non-infrastructure cell, so widening its rethrow to every
// error (the gate's D-TW-GET) reddened 0 of 751 cells. These cells feed query-level pg errors to the review read.
import { describe, it, expect, beforeAll, afterAll, beforeEach, vi } from 'vitest';
import type { AddressInfo } from 'node:net';
import type { Server } from 'node:http';
import express from 'express';

const state = vi.hoisted(() => ({
  caller: { userId: 'ks1193-admin', role: 'SYSTEM_ADMIN', tenantId: 'tenant-default' },
  users: new Map<string, Record<string, unknown>>(),
}));
const dbQuery = vi.hoisted(() => vi.fn());

vi.mock('../repositories/userRepo', () => {
  const repoMock = {
    getUserById: vi.fn(async (id: string) => state.users.get(id) ?? null),
    getUserByIdPlatformScope: vi.fn(async (id: string) => state.users.get(id) ?? null),
    updateUser: vi.fn(async () => null),
    updateUserPlatformScope: vi.fn(async () => null),
    listUsers: vi.fn(async () => []),
    countUsers: vi.fn(async () => 0),
    listOrganizations: vi.fn(async () => []),
    getOrganizationTenant: vi.fn(async () => ({ tenantId: 'tenant-default' })),
    emailExists: vi.fn(async () => false),
    createUser: vi.fn(async (user: Record<string, unknown>) => user),
    createInvitedStub: vi.fn(async () => ({ user: {}, alreadyExisted: false })),
  };
  return { default: repoMock, __esModule: true, ...repoMock };
});

vi.mock('../middleware/authenticate', () => {
  const injectCaller =
    () => (req: express.Request & { user?: unknown }, _res: express.Response, next: express.NextFunction) => {
      (req as { user: unknown }).user = { ...state.caller };
      next();
    };
  return { authenticate: injectCaller, authenticateAccessOrConnector: injectCaller };
});

vi.mock('../db', () => ({ isDbAvailable: () => true, query: dbQuery }));
vi.mock('../services/session', () => ({ revokeAllUserSessions: vi.fn(async () => 0) }));
vi.mock('../services/password', () => ({ hashPassword: vi.fn(async () => 'hashed'), checkPasswordStrength: vi.fn(() => ({ ok: true })), verifyPassword: vi.fn(async () => true) }));
vi.mock('../utils/logger', () => ({ logger: { info: vi.fn(), warn: vi.fn(), error: vi.fn(), debug: vi.fn() } }));

import { userRoutes } from '../routes/users';
import { errorHandler } from '../middleware/errorHandler';
import { logger } from '../utils/logger';
import * as userRepo from '../repositories/userRepo';

const GET_SQL = 'WHERE id = '; // only the review by-id read carries this
const MISSING_TABLE = Object.assign(new Error('relation verification_upgrade_requests does not exist'), { code: '42P01' });
const SERIALIZATION = Object.assign(new Error('could not serialize access due to concurrent update'), { code: '40001' });

let server: Server;
let baseUrl = '';

/** Every statement answers healthily, except the review by-id read, which rejects with this error. */
function failReviewRead(err: Error): void {
  dbQuery.mockImplementation(async (sql: string) => {
    if (sql.includes(GET_SQL)) throw err;
    return { rows: [] };
  });
}

async function review(requestId: string): Promise<{ status: number; code: unknown }> {
  const res = await fetch(baseUrl + '/verification/' + requestId + '/review', { method: 'POST', headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' }, body: JSON.stringify({ action: 'approve' }) });
  const json = (await res.json()) as { error?: { code?: unknown } };
  return { status: res.status, code: json.error?.code };
}

function writes(): number[] {
  const inserts = dbQuery.mock.calls.filter((c) => String(c[0]).includes('INSERT INTO verification_upgrade_requests')).length;
  return [vi.mocked(userRepo.updateUserPlatformScope).mock.calls.length, inserts];
}

beforeAll(async () => {
  const app = express();
  app.use(express.json());
  app.use('/', userRoutes);
  app.use(errorHandler);
  server = app.listen(0, '127.0.0.1');
  await new Promise<void>((resolve) => server.once('listening', () => resolve()));
  baseUrl = 'http://127.0.0.1:' + String((server.address() as AddressInfo).port);
});

afterAll(async () => { await new Promise<void>((resolve) => server.close(() => resolve())); });

beforeEach(() => {
  vi.clearAllMocks();
  state.caller = { userId: 'ks1193-admin', role: 'SYSTEM_ADMIN', tenantId: 'tenant-default' };
  dbQuery.mockResolvedValue({ rows: [] });
});

describe('KS-1193 F2 — a query-level pg error on the review read is not an availability failure', () => {
  it('🔴 KS-1193 F2 — review as SYSTEM_ADMIN: a 42P01 missing relation on the by-id read is not answered 503', async () => {
    failReviewRead(MISSING_TABLE);
    const res = await review('ks1193-unknown-request-a');
    expect(res.code, 'a query-level error is not an availability failure').not.toBe('SERVICE_UNAVAILABLE');
    expect([404, 500], 'the fall-through 404, or a KS-253 500').toContain(res.status);
    expect(writes(), 'no level raised, no row written').toEqual([0, 0]);
  });

  it('🔴 KS-1193 F2 — review as SYSTEM_ADMIN: a 40001 serialization failure on the by-id read is not answered 503', async () => {
    failReviewRead(SERIALIZATION);
    const res = await review('ks1193-unknown-request-b');
    expect(res.code, 'a query-level error is not an availability failure').not.toBe('SERVICE_UNAVAILABLE');
    expect([404, 500], 'the fall-through 404, or a KS-253 500').toContain(res.status);
    expect(writes(), 'no level raised, no row written').toEqual([0, 0]);
  });

  it('🟢 KS-1193 control — the 42P01 reaches the review read: its catch logs the line once with that code', async () => {
    failReviewRead(MISSING_TABLE);
    await review('ks1193-unknown-request-c');
    const calls = vi.mocked(logger.error).mock.calls as unknown as Array<[string, Record<string, unknown>]>;
    expect(calls.filter((c) => c[0] === 'DB getVerificationRequest failed').map((c) => c[1].code), 'the review read').toEqual(['42P01']);
  });
});
```

Why each cell is what it is:
- **🔴 42P01** (a missing relation, pg class 42) and **🔴 40001** (a serialization failure, pg class 40): neither is an
  infrastructure error in `dbErrors.ts`. A SYSTEM_ADMIN approves an unknown request while only the review by-id read
  rejects. The answer's `error.code` must not be `SERVICE_UNAVAILABLE`, its status must be 404 (today's fall-through:
  unknown id → `NotFoundError`) or 500 (the KS-253 propagate-as-500 contract in `dbErrors.ts`'s doc comment), and
  `writes()` must be `[0, 0]`. The cells accept either status on purpose: gate finding F4 (whether a query-level error on
  these reads should fall through at all) is still open, and this pin must not take a side on it. It pins only that such
  an error is never an availability failure.
- Under the tamper (the review rethrow widened to every error, the gate's D-TW-GET) both 🔴 cells fail **by assertion**
  on their first line: `a query-level error is not an availability failure: expected 'SERVICE_UNAVAILABLE' not to be
  'SERVICE_UNAVAILABLE'`.
- **🟢 control (hit witness):** the same 42P01 on the same read; it asserts only that the review read's catch logged
  `DB getVerificationRequest failed` exactly once with code `42P01`. Green under the tamper (the log line runs before the
  rethrow). Red if the fragment stops matching the by-id SELECT or the catch stops running, so it proves the 🔴 cells'
  error reaches the review read and not some other statement.

## Red cells

- 🔴 KS-1193 F2 — review as SYSTEM_ADMIN: a 42P01 missing relation on the by-id read is not answered 503
- 🔴 KS-1193 F2 — review as SYSTEM_ADMIN: a 40001 serialization failure on the by-id read is not answered 503

## Tamper (test-only mode — the checker plants this in a clone to prove your cells can go red)

line: 1186
from: `      if (isInfrastructureDbError(err)) throw new ServiceUnavailableError('Authentication service temporarily unavailable, please retry');`
to: `      if (err) throw new ServiceUnavailableError('Authentication service temporarily unavailable, please retry');`
statement_ok: the `from` line is one complete `if (…) throw …;` statement; the builder flags it only because the next line (`:1187`) is `    }`, which CLOSES the catch block (`:1184-1187`) and does not continue the statement. That catch holds only the log line (`:1185`) and this rethrow, so nothing after `:1186` in the catch can veto the widened throw; the function's fall-through (`:1189`) is reached only when nothing was thrown. `to` is the gate's D-TW-GET text from its `tampers.py`, byte-for-byte. The same text also sits at `:1210` and `:1235` (the other two reads); the checker plants by line number, so only the review read changes. Project tsc rc 0.

## Output

Exactly ONE ```diff block containing ONLY the new test file: `--- /dev/null` with NO leading space, then
`+++ b/Blockchain/Dev/services/auth/src/__tests__/ks1193-review-read-query-error-is-not-503.test.ts`, then
`@@ -0,0 +1,117 @@`, then the 117 lines above, each prefixed with `+`. Blank lines are a bare `+`. No hunk for
`users.ts` or any other file.

